From 9caf3c2b44a5ebf5d0b94f6a0d2718a7be32ff8a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kartheek Palla <palla.kartheekreddy@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 15:46:07 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] feat(oid4vc-service): let an issuer advertise only its
 own credentials
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

credential-schema's /oid4vci-configs is deployment-wide: it returns every
published config and takes no filter. With one issuer that is invisible. With two
issuers sharing a schema service it is wrong — each one's metadata lists the
other's credentials, so a wallet's issuer directory shows the same credential
under whichever issuer the holder happened to open, and an issuer appears to offer
credentials it cannot actually issue.

Found while standing up two registries against one deployment: both advertised
all three published credentials, including the unrelated one belonging to a third
issuer.

A schema already carries the DID that authored it — the same value this service
already uses as the per-schema issuer DID when creating an offer — so an issuer can
recognise its own without any new concept. ADVERTISE_OWN_CREDENTIALS_ONLY filters
on it.

Off by default. A single-issuer deployment upgrading to this build sees no change
at all, which is the only reason it is a flag rather than the behaviour.

It also requires ISSUER_DID to be set: filtering on an empty DID would advertise
nothing, which is a worse failure than advertising too much because it looks
exactly like the schema service being down. When the flag is on and nothing
matches, the service warns and names the mismatch rather than going quiet — an
issuer advertising no credentials is otherwise indistinguishable from a broken
dependency.
